Z||000004||theogre||01-26-2001||08:32 PM||||I'm working on this myself. There are a couple things to keep in mind....<P>How are you planning to control it? It will likely need at least a relay. These fans usually draw more current than you can just pull off an existing switched line. <P>Most OE radiator fans are pullers. The fan blades are designed to work best in one way. You can't just spin them backward to reverse flow. You might be able to find a small aftermarket fan that is a pusher.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||geyser||01-26-2001||11:32 PM||||Ogre, Perma-Cool lists 10" puller/pusher fan with dimensions of 13 x 10.5 x 2.5. This would fit under one of the removable vents. Control can be manual or temp. sensor. The fan is rated at 1250 cfm, plenty of blow for cooling. The fan draws 6.8 amps, max 15amps at start up. Part # 19010. ||||reg||3
Z||000006||terryk||01-26-2001||11:35 PM||||2 demerits Stugood for that bad pun!<P>I was looking at a pair of Geo fans. Small. Might fit nicely. Might not ...||||reg||12
Z||000007||theogre||01-26-2001||11:55 PM||||geyser, you will still need a relay to run it with anything but Permacool's adjustable thermostat.<P>Give me a couple days to post a cave update I have comming. It should help. It doesn't specifically cover an vent grate fan yet. That will be added once I have all details.<P>I have found a source for sealed fixed temperature thermostats that, with a relay, can be used to activate a fan based on engine bay temperature. (Cantherm in the Digikey catalog.) The update I have comming tells you how to alter the coolant and V6 aux fans. You could adapt the V6 circuit to the vent grate fan very easily. I should have a page specifically for a grate fan by next weekend.||||reg||1
Z||000008||geyser||01-27-2001||02:53 AM||||Ogre, thanks for the info. I will check the cave out. Have you tried "40 Below" coolant additive? It worked for my Dodge 440 but I have not tried it on the Fiero. ||||reg||6
Z||000009||theogre||01-27-2001||01:52 PM||||I've posted the new heat managment section. It's right at the top of the index. (And yes, it's on the WebTV index to.) In the interest of time I have not altered older versions of some article spread thru other seactions. I'll do that next week.<P>Here's the deal... You can use the circuit diagram for the V6 auxilary fan to connect a new fan in the engine bay. HOWEVER! You MUST NOT take the fan power from the FAN E fuse. Especially if you have the V6 fan already.<P>Instead get an inline fuse holder, the kind that is sealed and takes the same fuses Fiero already uses. Connect the fuse holder to the big terminal under C500 next to the battery. Then connect the fuse holder to the contacts of the new relay. You'll want a 20A fuse in the holder.<P>If you use tie the coil of the relay between the fan control line and ground the fan will come on when the key is on. <P>If you connect a new thermostat between the relay and ground then the fan will only come on if the car is on and the engine bay is hot. <P>If you get the thermostat from Digikey use one in the 75-85C range. you want the "normal open" ones "with leads" not "pc pins" these thermostat are sealed so you don't have to worry about crap getting n them. they are also small so they don't stant out like a sore.<P>I'll write all this up next week.<P>WHAT I NEED FROM YOU! I need to know what fan ends up fitting. Preferably I need a picture of it installed. I won't be able to put a fan in there untill the summer at least. I can write up the wiring but I'd really like to have a picture of one installed.||||reg||1
Z||000010||geyser||01-29-2001||01:32 AM||||Ogre, this project is still a few months away, but I will post pics when I get to it. Again, thanks for your info. ||||reg||6
Z||000011||theogre||01-29-2001||07:18 PM||||I had some time to kill.... I posted the wiring for what I think is the best way to set it up. There are other ways, I like this because the behavior ends up more OE.||||reg||1
